WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.145
Rust's unsafe keyword marks code where the
programmer must uphold rules the compiler

2
00:00:04.225 --> 00:00:05.085
cannot fully check.

3
00:00:05.165 --> 00:00:08.506
Unsafe code is sometimes necessary for
performance, low-level systems work,

4
00:00:08.586 --> 00:00:12.433
hardware interaction, custom abstractions,
or interoperability with other languages.

5
00:00:12.513 --> 00:00:14.336
The keyword does not mean the code is
wrong,

6
00:00:14.416 --> 00:00:16.188
but it does mean the review model changes.

7
00:00:16.268 --> 00:00:19.204
Unsafe blocks should be rare, isolated,
justified, and documented.

8
00:00:19.284 --> 00:00:22.776
The goal is to keep the trusted surface
small enough that reviewers can understand

9
00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:23.008
it.

10
00:00:23.088 --> 00:00:26.581
When possible, unsafe internals should be
wrapped behind safe APIs that preserve

11
00:00:26.661 --> 00:00:27.724
documented invariants.

12
00:00:27.804 --> 00:00:31.449
Callers should not need to understand raw
pointer rules or allocation contracts just

13
00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:33.301
to use an ordinary high-level operation.

14
00:00:33.381 --> 00:00:35.001
FFI boundaries require special care.

15
00:00:35.081 --> 00:00:38.118
Code that crosses into another language
may involve pointer validity,

16
00:00:38.198 --> 00:00:41.134
ownership transfer, allocation and
deallocation responsibility,

17
00:00:41.214 --> 00:00:43.947
thread assumptions, error conventions,
data representation,

18
00:00:44.027 --> 00:00:46.660
and lifetime expectations that Rust cannot
verify on its own.

19
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.283
Those assumptions should be written down
and tested, not left as tribal knowledge.

20
00:00:50.363 --> 00:00:52.590
The goal is not to forbid unsafe code
categorically.

21
00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.151
The goal is to prevent unsafe assumptions
from spreading.

22
00:00:55.231 --> 00:00:57.711
A good boundary explains what must be true
before the call,

23
00:00:57.791 --> 00:01:00.373
what the call guarantees afterward, who
owns returned data,

24
00:01:00.453 --> 00:01:03.338
how errors are represented, and which
tests exercise the boundary.

25
00:01:03.418 --> 00:01:07.013
Small, documented, reviewable unsafe areas
let the rest of the code remain easier to

26
00:01:07.093 --> 00:01:07.700
reason about.
